---
// About page "How I got here" (PROJECT_BRIEF.md Section 4/5, item 2): the
// copywriting-to-marketing-to-UX origin story, including one pull-quote.
// Copy is final, supplied directly by the user - kept close to verbatim;
// nothing here reads as excessive for a recruiter, so this is light
// tightening, not a rewrite. Two em dashes in the original copy are
// rewritten as separate sentences/a colon below - CLAUDE.md's own quality
// guardrails already ban em dashes in copy, this just brings About in
// line with a rule the page wasn't actually following yet.
//
// The pull-quote is this page's one Instrument Serif italic moment
// (Section 3: "once per page maximum"). It also carries this page's one
// macro-level grid break (Section 3 principle: "asymmetry at the macro
// level only") - it sits wider than the body column instead of staying
// inside it, the classic editorial "the quote breaks the column" move.
//
// Brutalist-structure pass (explicit request, About-only scope): the
// section's top hairline (a repeated "plate boundary" rule across all
// four About sections) is declared once here via :global() - that
// reaches every .section-marker on THIS page (Astro only ships a
// component's styles to pages that import it, so this never loads on the
// homepage bundle and never touches the shared SectionMarker.astro file
// itself). The SectionMarker index's own hairline box, part of that same
// pass, was later removed again (direct follow-up request, "remove the
// squares around the (01) etc") - the index now stays whatever
// SectionMarker.astro's own default styling gives it, same as the
// homepage.
//
// Chapter pass (explicit request, "add subtitles and subtle delicate
// chapter names to make the story feel more like a story... does it have
// to be straight up and down or do we travel across the page gently"):
// the five paragraphs (plus the closing two) used to sit in one straight
// vertical column. They're now five small "beats," each with a delicate
// chapter caption above it - a plain mono index + an italic body-font
// name, not Instrument Serif (that's still spent once, on the quote
// alone) - and each beat's own column range in the 12-col grid alternates
// left/right, so the eye drifts gently across the page reading down
// instead of tracking one dead-straight edge. The quote stays the
// section's one biggest, most dramatic break (`1 / 11`, unchanged); the
// beats' own drift is deliberately smaller than that so nothing competes
// with it. Collapses back to a single straight column below 1024px, same
// "macro asymmetry drops at a width threshold" pattern used everywhere
// else in this project - a zigzag reading path needs room to zig.
// Copy itself is untouched, split only at its own existing sentence/
// paragraph breaks - no line was rewritten to make the grouping work.
//
// Timeline + cascade + draggable-asset pass (direct request: "the
// paragraphs to appear one by one on scroll, with a soft timeline on the
// left as you reach a new chapter... paragraphs should gently alternate
// in alignment [already true, above], with placeholders for fun assets
// supporting the story from the paragraph... these images will be
// draggable with gsap, so already set that up"):
//
// - Each beat's own reveal changed from one fade/rise on the whole block
//   to a cascade through its own children (chapter caption, then each
//   paragraph in turn) via a single staggered tween - "appear one by
//   one," not all four/five words of a two-paragraph beat arriving at
//   once.
// - `.about-story__rail` is a new absolutely-positioned vertical line
//   sitting in the left gutter (outside the 12-col grid, so it never
//   competes with the quote's own column-1 start) - a faint full-height
//   track plus a solid progress overlay that grows via a scrubbed
//   ScrollTrigger tied to the whole chapters block's own scroll range.
//   Its `top`/`height` are measured in the script (not hardcoded), since
//   SectionMarker's own rendered height varies and the rail should start
//   exactly at the first chapter, not "roughly after the title."
// - `.about-story__asset` - one small placeholder per beat, "supporting
//   the story from the paragraph" - sits in whichever columns that
//   beat's own zigzag range leaves free (same "explicit grid-column,
//   shares its sibling's implicit row" technique already proven by the
//   quote/image pair below), alternating sides rather than defaulting to
//   one edge every time. Registered with GSAP's Draggable (see
//   utils/motion.ts) so they can be nudged around - a direct, literal
//   request to "already set that up," not gated behind
//   prefers-reduced-motion (dragging is user-initiated interaction, not
//   autoplaying motion; only the entrance settle-in below is gated).
//
// Follow-up refinement pass, several direct requests in one message:
//
// - The rail is thinner (2px -> 1px), and its faint always-visible
//   "track" backing line is gone outright - "the black line is on top
//   of the gray line, but I don't want to see the gray line." There's
//   nothing left to see ahead of where the reader has scrolled, which is
//   the intended read now: the line exists only as far as the story has
//   actually progressed.
// - Each chapter gets a small `.about-story__chapter-tick` - "a gentle
//   line extends from the timeline to the subtitle" - a short horizontal
//   mark, generated and positioned in the script (one per beat, aligned
//   to that beat's own chapter-caption row), revealed alongside that
//   beat's own cascade. Deliberately a fixed short length rather than
//   reaching all the way to the caption text ("not fully connected on
//   both sides") - a gesture toward the subtitle, not a technical
//   connector diagram; it also sidesteps needing to compute each
//   zigzagging beat's own exact horizontal text position.
// - Mobile no longer drops the rail/assets outright ("on mobile screens
//   it's looking kind of boring, find a way to still get the
//   storytelling in there") - both stay, just adapted: the rail sits
//   closer to the (now single-column) text edge, and each asset falls
//   into normal document flow as its own small stacked image between
//   beats instead of sharing a row via an explicit grid-column (there's
//   no "leftover column" to share once beats go full-width). The ticks
//   are the one piece that stays desktop-only - they're sized against
//   the zigzag's own leftover space, which doesn't exist once the
//   column collapses to one.
// - Draggable now uses real GSAP inertia - "use gsap inertia so the user
//   can flick and have the motion decelerate smoothly based on
//   momentum" - via `inertia: true` plus `InertiaPlugin`, newly
//   registered in utils/motion.ts. This used to need GSAP's paid Club
//   bonus plugin; it's part of the regular free `gsap` package now
//   (Webflow's acquisition made all of GSAP's plugins free), so no
//   separate license or CDN token was needed to wire it up.
//
// Beat 05's closing line ("Working at a grocery store has unexpectedly
// become one of my favourite places to observe human behaviour.") is
// removed per direct request, once it became redundant with a new
// second paragraph added to AboutIntro.astro covering the same ground
// (customer service jobs, everyday moments as where inspiration
// strikes) - "you can therefore remove the last sentence of 05 still
// noticing."
import SectionMarker from '../ui/SectionMarker.astro';
---

<section class="about-story">
  <div class="about-story__inner">
    <SectionMarker index="02" label="How I got here" />

    <div class="about-story__chapters">
      <div class="about-story__rail" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="about-story__rail-progress"></span>
      </div>

      <div class="about-story__beat about-story__beat--a">
        <p class="about-story__chapter">
          <span class="about-story__chapter-index" aria-hidden="true">
            01
          </span>
          <span class="about-story__chapter-name">Before design</span>
        </p>
        <p>Before design, I thought I wanted to become a copywriter.</p>
      </div>
      <span
        class="about-story__asset about-story__asset--1"
        aria-hidden="true"
        data-rest-rotate="3"
      ></span>

      <div class="about-story__beat about-story__beat--b">
        <p class="about-story__chapter">
          <span class="about-story__chapter-index" aria-hidden="true">
            02
          </span>
          <span class="about-story__chapter-name">Finding my voice</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          I've always been a storyteller. As a kid, I used to write little stories about my sister's
          favourite Nintendo characters just to convince her to read. It worked.
        </p>
        <p>
          That instinct never really disappeared. I still love turning ideas into stories, finding
          creative angles and figuring out how to communicate something in a way that makes people
          care.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span
        class="about-story__asset about-story__asset--2"
        aria-hidden="true"
        data-rest-rotate="-4"
      ></span>

      <div class="about-story__beat about-story__beat--a">
        <p class="about-story__chapter">
          <span class="about-story__chapter-index" aria-hidden="true">
            03
          </span>
          <span class="about-story__chapter-name">Into marketing</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          That eventually led me to marketing communication, where I discovered that a campaign
          isn't just a creative execution. It's a creative response to a real problem.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span
        class="about-story__asset about-story__asset--3"
        aria-hidden="true"
        data-rest-rotate="-2"
      ></span>

      <div class="about-story__beat about-story__beat--b">
        <p class="about-story__chapter">
          <span class="about-story__chapter-index" aria-hidden="true">
            04
          </span>
          <span class="about-story__chapter-name">Toward UX</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          From there, I became increasingly interested in what happens beyond communication: how
          people experience products, services and systems themselves. That's what pulled me toward
          UX and experience design.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span
        class="about-story__asset about-story__asset--4"
        aria-hidden="true"
        data-rest-rotate="4"
      ></span>

      <blockquote class="about-story__quote">
        <span class="about-story__quote-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>I turned my tendency to
        overthink into a habit of observing, questioning and challenging assumptions.
      </blockquote>

      <span class="about-story__image" aria-hidden="true">
        <!-- TODO: replace with a real documentary/process image once photography exists (PROJECT_BRIEF.md Section 6) -->
      </span>

      <div class="about-story__beat about-story__beat--c">
        <p class="about-story__chapter">
          <span class="about-story__chapter-index" aria-hidden="true">
            05
          </span>
          <span class="about-story__chapter-name">Still noticing</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          My brain rarely leaves things alone. A confusing website, an inefficient queue, a
          frustrated customer: I immediately start wondering what could make the experience better.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span
        class="about-story__asset about-story__asset--5"
        aria-hidden="true"
        data-rest-rotate="-3"
      ></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import { gsap, Draggable, prefersReducedMotion } from '../../utils/motion';

  const reduceMotion = prefersReducedMotion();

  const quote = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.about-story__quote');
  const rule = quote?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.about-story__quote-rule');
  if (quote && rule && !reduceMotion) {
    // The one emphasis-reveal on this page: the rule draws down first, like
    // a mark being ruled onto a page, then the text settles in behind it.
    // Distinct from the plain fade/rise used elsewhere, marking this as the
    // section's one deliberate beat rather than a third repeat of the same
    // recipe - and more mechanical-precision than a soft scale/fade, which
    // suits this page's more structural character better.
    gsap.set(rule, { scaleY: 0 });
    gsap.set(quote, { autoAlpha: 0, y: 16 });

    gsap
      .timeline({ scrollTrigger: { trigger: quote, start: 'top 85%' } })
      .to(rule, { scaleY: 1, duration: 0.5, ease: 'power4.out' })
      .to(quote, { autoAlpha: 1, y: 0, duration: 0.6, ease: 'power2.out' }, 0.2);
  }

  // Timeline rail setup happens before the beat loop below, since each
  // beat's own chapter-tick (created here, one per beat) needs to exist
  // before that loop can reveal it alongside the beat's own cascade.
  const chapters = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.about-story__chapters');
  const rail = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.about-story__rail');
  const railProgress = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.about-story__rail-progress');
  const beats = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.about-story__beat');
  const firstBeat = beats[0];
  const lastBeat = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.about-story__beat--c');

  // One tick per beat - "a gentle line extends from the timeline to the
  // subtitle" - created here rather than hardcoded per beat in the
  // markup, so its position logic lives in one place next to the rail's
  // own. A fixed short length (set in CSS), not computed against each
  // beat's own zigzagging caption position - "not fully connected on
  // both sides," a gesture toward the subtitle, not a drawn connector.
  const ticks: HTMLElement[] = [];
  if (chapters) {
    beats.forEach(() => {
      const tick = document.createElement('span');
      tick.className = 'about-story__chapter-tick';
      tick.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      chapters.appendChild(tick);
      ticks.push(tick);
    });
  }

  if (chapters && rail && railProgress && firstBeat && lastBeat) {
    const positionRail = () => {
      // offsetTop is already relative to .about-story__chapters itself
      // (it's `position: relative`, making it the offsetParent for its
      // descendants) - subtracting chapters.offsetTop again would double
      // that up against chapters' own distance from *its* offsetParent,
      // producing a wildly wrong (and often negative) top. A real bug,
      // caught by measuring the rendered rail's rect directly rather
      // than trusting the arithmetic read correctly.
      const top = firstBeat.offsetTop;
      const bottom = lastBeat.offsetTop + lastBeat.offsetHeight;
      rail.style.top = `${top}px`;
      rail.style.height = `${bottom - top}px`;

      // Each tick centers on its own beat's chapter-caption line -
      // measured fresh here too, since it depends on the same layout
      // (font metrics, column width) the rail itself depends on.
      beats.forEach((beat, i) => {
        const caption = beat.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.about-story__chapter');
        const tick = ticks[i];
        if (!caption || !tick) return;
        // Same fix as the rail's own top/bottom above: caption.offsetTop
        // is already relative to .about-story__chapters (its
        // offsetParent, since .about-story__beat itself isn't
        // positioned), so no second subtraction here either.
        tick.style.top = `${caption.offsetTop + caption.offsetHeight / 2}px`;
      });
    };

    positionRail();
    window.addEventListener('resize', positionRail);

    if (!reduceMotion) {
      gsap.set(railProgress, { scaleY: 0 });
      gsap.to(railProgress, {
        scaleY: 1,
        ease: 'none',
        scrollTrigger: {
          trigger: rail,
          start: 'top 75%',
          end: 'bottom 60%',
          scrub: 0.3,
        },
      });
    } else {
      gsap.set(railProgress, { scaleY: 1 });
    }
  }

  // Each beat now cascades through its own children (chapter caption,
  // then each paragraph) rather than fading in as one block - "the
  // paragraphs to appear one by one on scroll." A single staggered tween
  // per beat, not a separate ScrollTrigger per paragraph: they all still
  // trigger together as the beat scrolls into view, just arriving a beat
  // apart from each other instead of simultaneously. Each beat's own
  // tick (above) reveals alongside it on the same trigger.
  if (beats.length && !reduceMotion) {
    beats.forEach((beat, i) => {
      const items = Array.from(beat.children) as HTMLElement[];
      gsap.set(items, { autoAlpha: 0, y: 20 });
      gsap.to(items, {
        autoAlpha: 1,
        y: 0,
        duration: 0.6,
        ease: 'power2.out',
        stagger: 0.15,
        scrollTrigger: { trigger: beat, start: 'top 90%' },
      });

      // Scrubbed, not a discrete on/off threshold like the text above -
      // per direct feedback that the ticks "should also appear on
      // scroll, not just be there statically." A fixed-duration tween
      // on the same 'top 90%' threshold as the text was technically
      // scroll-triggered, but most beats in this section sit close
      // enough together that several of their thresholds clear within
      // one short scroll, so four of the five ticks could already be at
      // their end state the moment the section came into view - reading
      // as "just there" rather than something that happened in response
      // to scrolling. Tying it to `scrub` instead makes the growth
      // track the scrollbar directly, the same technique already used
      // for the rail's own progress line above.
      const tick = ticks[i];
      if (tick) {
        gsap.set(tick, { scaleX: 0 });
        gsap.to(tick, {
          scaleX: 1,
          ease: 'none',
          scrollTrigger: { trigger: beat, start: 'top 95%', end: 'top 55%', scrub: 0.3 },
        });
      }
    });
  } else if (beats.length) {
    gsap.set(ticks, { scaleX: 1 });
  }

  // Fun assets: a small settle-in on scroll (skipped under reduced
  // motion, same as every other entrance on this page), then registered
  // with GSAP's Draggable regardless of reduced motion - dragging is a
  // direct, user-initiated action, not autoplaying motion, so it isn't
  // something prefers-reduced-motion is meant to suppress. Each asset's
  // own resting tilt comes from its `data-rest-rotate` attribute (see
  // the markup) rather than a CSS custom property, since the entrance
  // tween needs that exact number as its animation target too.
  const assets = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.about-story__asset');
  if (assets.length) {
    assets.forEach((asset) => {
      const restRotate = Number(asset.dataset.restRotate ?? '0');
      if (!reduceMotion) {
        gsap.set(asset, { autoAlpha: 0, scale: 0.9, rotate: restRotate - 8 });
        gsap.to(asset, {
          autoAlpha: 1,
          scale: 1,
          rotate: restRotate,
          duration: 0.7,
          ease: 'back.out(1.6)',
          scrollTrigger: { trigger: asset, start: 'top 92%' },
        });
      } else {
        gsap.set(asset, { rotate: restRotate });
      }
    });

    // inertia: true - "flick and have the motion decelerate smoothly
    // based on momentum" - hands off to InertiaPlugin (registered in
    // utils/motion.ts) on release, which reads the pointer's recent
    // velocity and keeps the asset moving with its own natural
    // deceleration instead of just stopping dead where the pointer let
    // go. Still bounded to the section, so a hard flick can't send it
    // off-page - InertiaPlugin respects Draggable's own `bounds` and
    // eases to a stop against the edge rather than clipping through it.
    Draggable.create(assets, {
      type: 'x,y',
      bounds: '.about-story__inner',
      edgeResistance: 0.65,
      inertia: true,
      cursor: 'grab',
      activeCursor: 'grabbing',
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* The repeated top hairline that used to sit here (and on AboutProcess/
     AboutDrives) - a "plate boundary" turning section padding into a
     visible dossier-page break - is gone per direct request ("remove
     the lines in between the sections on the about me page"). */
  .about-story {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
  }

  .about-story__inner {
    max-width: var(--intro-max-width);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--intro-margin);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--space-24);
    row-gap: var(--space-32);
  }

  .about-story__inner > :global(.section-marker) {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  /* Wraps everything below the title - the rail (below) measures its own
     top/height against this element specifically, not the whole
     .about-story__inner (which also contains SectionMarker), and it's
     what the rail's ScrollTrigger scrubs against. Stays a grid itself
     (not a nested independent grid) so beats/quote/image/assets keep
     sharing the same 12 columns and the existing row-sharing tricks
     (quote+image, beat+asset) keep working exactly as before. */
  .about-story__chapters {
    position: relative;
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--space-24);
    row-gap: var(--space-32);
  }

  /* Sits in the left gutter, outside the 12-col grid - deliberately not
     column 1, which the quote (grid-column: 1/11) also starts flush
     against further down. top/height are set from the script (measured
     against the first/last beat), not hardcoded, since SectionMarker's
     own height varies. No permanent "track" backing line anymore - per
     direct request ("the black line is on top of the gray line, but I
     don't want to see the gray line") - so there's nothing rendered
     ahead of wherever the reader has actually scrolled to. */
  .about-story__rail {
    position: absolute;
    left: -1.5rem;
    width: 1px;
  }

  .about-story__rail-progress {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: var(--color-ink);
    transform-origin: top;
  }

  /* "A gentle line extends from the timeline to the subtitle" - a short
     mark near (not touching) the rail on one end and the chapter
     caption on the other, generated and positioned per-beat in the
     script. Fixed length, not stretched to actually reach the caption -
     "not fully connected on both sides."

     :global() is required here, not a style preference: Astro scopes a
     component's CSS by stamping a data-astro-cid attribute onto every
     element that exists in its *server-rendered* markup, and a plain
     (non-global) selector compiles down to expect that attribute. These
     spans are created at runtime via document.createElement, so they
     never receive it - the scoped rule was silently matching nothing
     (position stayed the default `static`, so the ticks were rendering
     as ordinary auto-placed grid items instead of the positioned marks
     they're meant to be), confirmed via getComputedStyle rather than
     assumed from the CSS reading right. Same underlying Astro-scoping
     trap already documented elsewhere in this project (see Button.astro
     and Hero.astro's own comments on it), just triggered by a
     dynamically-created element instead of a class passed into another
     component. */
  :global(.about-story__chapter-tick) {
    position: absolute;
    left: -1rem;
    width: 2.25rem;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--color-text-secondary);
    transform-origin: left;
  }

  .about-story__beat {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    max-width: 60ch;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    line-height: var(--text-body-leading);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .about-story__beat > p:not(.about-story__chapter) {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* A delicate caption, not a second headline - plain mono index (same
     register as every numbered device sitewide) plus an italic body-font
     name. Deliberately not Instrument Serif - that stays this page's one
     italic moment, spent on the quote alone. */
  .about-story__chapter {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.6em;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .about-story__chapter-index {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .about-story__chapter-name {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  /* The zigzag: two alternating column ranges, both narrower than the
     quote's own 1/11 break so nothing competes with it. */
  .about-story__beat--a {
    grid-column: 2 / 8;
  }

  .about-story__beat--b {
    grid-column: 4 / 10;
  }

  /* The closing beat, after the quote - a smaller shift than a/b, reads
     as the drift settling back down rather than snapping to either
     earlier position. */
  .about-story__beat--c {
    grid-column: 3 / 9;
  }

  /* One small placeholder per beat, sized and pinned (align-self: start,
     a fixed aspect ratio rather than stretching to the row's own
     height) so it reads as a pinned photo, not a second column of
     content. Each sits in whichever columns its own beat's zigzag range
     leaves free that row - same explicit-grid-column/shared-implicit-row
     technique the quote+image pair below already relies on. Alternates
     sides rather than always falling to the same edge, and each gets its
     own small resting tilt (set from data-rest-rotate in the script) for
     the same "pinned by hand" read AboutProcess's own collage already
     uses. Grabbable via GSAP Draggable (see the script) - cursor: grab
     signals that before the user ever tries. */
  .about-story__asset {
    display: block;
    align-self: start;
    width: clamp(5rem, 9vw, 7.5rem);
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-text-secondary);
    cursor: grab;
    touch-action: none;
  }

  .about-story__asset--1 {
    grid-column: 9 / 12;
  }

  .about-story__asset--2 {
    grid-column: 1 / 4;
  }

  .about-story__asset--3 {
    grid-column: 9 / 12;
  }

  .about-story__asset--4 {
    grid-column: 1 / 4;
  }

  .about-story__asset--5 {
    grid-column: 10 / 13;
  }

  .about-story__quote {
    position: relative;
    grid-column: 1 / 11;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-32);
    font-family: var(--font-editorial);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 2vw + 1.25rem, 2.75rem);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Sits in the two columns the quote's own 1/11 span leaves open on the
     right, same row - a small documentary-style placeholder breaking up
     the quote's own macro-asymmetry move instead of duplicating it. */
  .about-story__image {
    display: block;
    grid-column: 11 / 13;
    align-self: start;
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 4;
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  /* A real element, not border-left: GSAP needs a node it can scale
